WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.480 --> 00:00:04.920
The attacks start quietly.

00:00:04.920 --> 00:00:09.800
Someone clicks on a link in an email — and
at first, nothing seems to happen.

00:00:09.800 --> 00:00:14.240
But that one click opens the door for intruders.

00:00:14.240 --> 00:00:23.910
And once the time is right, they strike, creating
chaos and confusion.

00:00:23.910 --> 00:00:27.130
But what if that system is needed to run an
entire country?

00:00:27.130 --> 00:00:36.340
And what can be done to protect it?

00:00:36.340 --> 00:00:42.460
From DW, this is Techtopia, and in this episode,
we are looking at how digital technology has

00:00:42.460 --> 00:00:47.600
become critical for running our societies
– and how this allows hackers to paralyze

00:00:47.600 --> 00:00:50.480
them in milliseconds.

00:00:50.480 --> 00:00:52.780
We explain how the attacks work.

00:00:52.780 --> 00:00:57.460
We have insights into a global network of
criminals who make billions by taking computers

00:00:57.460 --> 00:00:58.460
hostage.

00:00:58.460 --> 00:00:59.460
“It’s a booming industry.”

00:00:59.460 --> 00:01:04.440
And we show how governments can use similar
methods as a political weapon.

00:01:04.440 --> 00:01:09.040
And if you want to know what it’s like to
be in the middle of such an attack – listen

00:01:09.040 --> 00:01:17.070
to this:
This is Sabine Griebsch — and in the summer

00:01:17.070 --> 00:01:24.130
of 2021, her small hometown in central Germany
was hit by a form of cybercrime that rages

00:01:24.130 --> 00:01:47.150
in the shadows of 

00:01:47.150 --> 00:01:52.340
the internet.

00:01:52.340 --> 00:02:06.640
But the 
calls kept coming in, with employees from

00:02:06.640 --> 00:02:10.789
across the district complaining that their
computers were dead.

00:02:10.789 --> 00:02:16.409
Alarmed, the IT professionals combed through
the system — and came across a ransom note

00:02:16.409 --> 00:02:31.150
that made their blood curdle: “Landkreis
Anhalt – Bitterfeld, you are f***d.

00:02:31.150 --> 00:02:37.430
That’s when it started to dawn on them:
This wasn’t just another cyber attack.

00:02:37.430 --> 00:02:41.180
This was more serious.

00:02:41.180 --> 00:02:49.390
So: Who were the intruders?

00:02:49.390 --> 00:02:50.920
What did they want?

00:02:50.920 --> 00:02:53.150
And how did the district get out of this?

00:02:53.150 --> 00:02:54.909
We’ll have some answers.

00:02:54.909 --> 00:03:00.769
But first, let’s take a step back:
In many ways, our advanced societies today

00:03:00.769 --> 00:03:04.700
are like our human bodies, where thousands
of different processes take place simultaneously.

00:03:04.700 --> 00:03:10.810
And just like some organs are critical for
our survival, some infrastructure is so vital

00:03:10.810 --> 00:03:14.030
that everything would collapse without it.

00:03:14.030 --> 00:03:19.379
Traditionally, such “critical infrastructure”
is built so that it can run autonomously.

00:03:19.379 --> 00:03:31.950
But 
over the last two decades, a lot of it was

00:03:31.950 --> 00:03:39.420
connected to the internet, and now depends
on access to digital technology — as if

00:03:39.420 --> 00:03:43.900
a human body were connected to an artificial
external brain millions of times more powerful.

00:03:43.900 --> 00:03:50.209
This has quietly created an additional, invisible
layer of digital technology connecting our

00:03:50.209 --> 00:03:51.209
critical infrastructure.

00:03:51.209 --> 00:03:55.379
This helps make it run more efficiently than
ever — but it also makes it increasingly

00:03:55.379 --> 00:04:02.159
interdependent: Windmills in Germany, for
example, might need to be connected to American

00:04:02.159 --> 00:04:03.889
satellites to work properly.

00:04:03.889 --> 00:04:16.230
That’s why whenever this invisible layer
is disrupted, it can cause cascading effects

00:04:16.230 --> 00:04:43.660
and — in the worst-case scenario — spiral
out of control and knock out the critical

00:04:43.660 --> 00:04:51.060
infrastructure of an entire society.

00:04:51.060 --> 00:04:57.850
Entire offices, from social welfare to immigration
services, had to shut down — and as news

00:04:57.850 --> 00:05:08.790
about the blackout spread across the district,
so did residents’ fears.

00:05:08.790 --> 00:05:11.100
What happened?

00:05:11.100 --> 00:05:15.290
Within hours, authorities realized that they
had fallen victim to organized cybercrime,

00:05:15.290 --> 00:05:18.080
and what’s known as a “ransomware attack.”

00:05:18.080 --> 00:05:20.590
But how do these attacks work?

00:05:20.590 --> 00:05:27.410
They often follow a similar playbook: First,
intruders gain access to a computer network.

00:05:27.410 --> 00:05:32.290
Just as a virus finds different ways to enter
our bodies, hackers have an arsenal of tricks

00:05:32.290 --> 00:05:34.430
to get into the systems.

00:05:34.430 --> 00:05:39.830
Often, they disguise themselves as trusted
contacts and lure people into revealing their

00:05:39.830 --> 00:05:41.250
login data.

00:05:41.250 --> 00:05:47.440
Once inside, hackers can spend weeks or even
months snooping around – looking for information

00:05:47.440 --> 00:05:52.120
so valuable people would pay money to get
it back.

00:05:52.120 --> 00:05:56.020
When they find what they’re looking for,
they encrypt the data – and send a message

00:05:56.020 --> 00:06:01.650
to their victims, offering a decryption key
in return for a ransom.

00:06:01.650 --> 00:06:05.920
Victims are left with two options: to pay
up and trust that they get their data back

00:06:05.920 --> 00:06:11.930
— or to refuse, and hope that they can recover
some of the hijacked data.

00:06:11.930 --> 00:06:21.300
And slowly, they realized the extent of what
had happened:

00:06:21.300 --> 00:06:24.920
But how did the intruders manage to break
into the system?

00:06:24.920 --> 00:06:25.940
Where was the loophole they used to get in?

00:06:25.940 --> 00:06:29.170
Months earlier, an employee had clicked on
an infected link in an email and entered their

00:06:29.170 --> 00:06:30.170
password.

00:06:30.170 --> 00:06:34.940
That, from all appearances, was when the attackers
got into the system.

00:06:34.940 --> 00:06:39.560
“Ransomware attacks like in Anhalt-Bitterfeld
have become common in the last years, and

00:06:39.560 --> 00:06:41.790
it gets worse every year.

00:06:41.790 --> 00:06:43.120
It’s organized crime.”

00:06:43.120 --> 00:06:49.290
Here in Germany alone, we know of more than
one hundred communities and public institutions

00:06:49.290 --> 00:06:53.420
that have had their systems taken hostage
in the last years.

00:06:53.420 --> 00:06:57.510
“It’s a booming industry, and it’s an
industry that has managers, planners, developers.

00:06:57.510 --> 00:07:00.880
Criminal groups took advantage of the Covid
pandemic and the changes to all our lives

00:07:00.880 --> 00:07:03.750
to attack health care institutions, public
institutions, local authorities.”

00:07:03.750 --> 00:07:10.330
So what about the attack in Anhalt-Bitterfeld
– who was behind it, and what did they want

00:07:10.330 --> 00:07:13.960
That meant the district would have to cover
all costs for the recovery itself.

00:07:13.960 --> 00:07:20.630
We know that there are communities that have
paid a ransom to get their data back.

00:07:20.630 --> 00:07:24.430
But what if you’re unwilling to strike such
a deal, and do business with criminals, what

00:07:24.430 --> 00:07:25.430
else can you do?

00:07:25.430 --> 00:07:53.900
In Anhalt-Bitterfeld, officials went through
all the options they had — and then, they

00:07:53.900 --> 00:10:10.910
decided to do something no one else had ever
done before:

00:10:10.910 --> 00:10:35.300
For the first time in German history, authorities
declared a state of emergency because of 

00:10:35.300 --> 00:10:54.490
a cyber attack.

00:10:54.490 --> 00:11:16.040
This meant, among other things, that Germany’s
armed forces could send in their cyber experts

00:11:16.040 --> 00:11:26.480
to help officials regain control over 
the system.

00:11:26.480 --> 00:11:28.750
And yet, their fight was far from over.

00:11:28.750 --> 00:11:34.519
We’ll get back to that – but first, let’s
take a look at the larger picture: What if

00:11:34.519 --> 00:11:39.839
attackers managed to bring down not just a
small community but the critical infrastructure

00:11:39.839 --> 00:11:43.029
of an entire country?

00:11:43.029 --> 00:11:49.050
Hackers have tried it before: In 2019, attackers
broke into the network of India’s largest

00:11:49.050 --> 00:11:50.860
nuclear power station.

00:11:50.860 --> 00:11:56.689
In 2021, an attack on a pipeline provider
led to panic buying and gas shortages in the

00:11:56.689 --> 00:11:57.689
US.

00:11:57.689 --> 00:12:03.680
And in 2022, hackers crippled the computer
systems of the government of Costa Rica.

00:12:03.680 --> 00:12:10.720
“Each of these incidents had an impact locally,
in the country or region where it happened.

00:12:10.720 --> 00:12:18.589
However, should an adversary, a cyber attack
group, decide to launch several attacks at

00:12:18.589 --> 00:12:24.540
the same time simultaneously in many different
countries around the world, the impact of

00:12:24.540 --> 00:12:28.309
that would be potentially devastating.”

00:12:28.309 --> 00:12:33.209
And what if attackers aren’t only in it
for the money: Could state actors use similar

00:12:33.209 --> 00:12:38.629
methods as a political weapon, to sow chaos
and weaken their enemy?

00:12:38.629 --> 00:12:39.629
“Well definitely.

00:12:39.629 --> 00:12:44.649
That is really a problem, because if criminals
cooperate with secret services, that is a

00:12:44.649 --> 00:12:46.869
kind of explosive combination.”

00:12:46.869 --> 00:12:51.619
To be clear: It’s incredibly difficult to
say with one hundred percent accuracy who’s

00:12:51.619 --> 00:12:53.629
behind a cyber attack.

00:12:53.629 --> 00:12:58.720
But the experts we talked to said that during
the early days of the war in Ukraine, there

00:12:58.720 --> 00:13:05.510
were incidents that could be examples for
a cooperation between state actors and criminals:

00:13:05.510 --> 00:13:11.339
“Literally, as Russian tanks were rolling
into Ukraine, we also saw digital components

00:13:11.339 --> 00:13:16.449
to the attacks — including an attack on
a satellite network, one that is even used

00:13:16.449 --> 00:13:19.019
by wind turbines in Germany.”

00:13:19.019 --> 00:13:24.760
And the war in Ukraine has also triggered
conflict within the criminal ransomware ecosystem.

00:13:24.760 --> 00:13:25.760
Confused?

00:13:25.760 --> 00:13:30.939
What’s key to understand is that before
the invasion, Russian ransomware groups often

00:13:30.939 --> 00:13:32.980
had operatives working in Ukraine.

00:13:32.980 --> 00:13:39.379
“As the conflict rolled out, these Ukrainian
operatives were not happy with what the cyber

00:13:39.379 --> 00:13:41.100
ransomware gangs were doing.

00:13:41.100 --> 00:13:46.189
So they decided to leak information, to publish
details, and these details will eventually

00:13:46.189 --> 00:13:51.999
help law enforcement in Europe and around
the world to track down and stop these gangs.”

00:13:51.999 --> 00:13:55.559
And those attacks can have an impact on every
one of us.

00:13:55.559 --> 00:14:01.839
Which brings us back from the stage of world
politics to the German district of Anhalt-Bitterfeld.

00:14:01.839 --> 00:14:05.649
Remember that authorities decided not to pay
a ransom?

00:14:05.649 --> 00:14:20.910
In the days that followed that decision, they
started to feel 

00:14:20.910 --> 00:14:34.920
the consequences:
The files included sensitive information such

00:14:34.920 --> 00:14:40.510
as bank account details or the employment
status of over 90 individuals.

00:14:40.510 --> 00:14:44.429
We tried to contact some of them — but none
of them wanted to appear on camera because

00:14:44.429 --> 00:14:45.749
of concerns over what happened to their data.

00:14:45.749 --> 00:15:05.350
Meanwhile, the administration’s standstill
sent unexpected ripple effects across 

00:15:05.350 --> 00:15:15.449
the district:
That’s why after the attack knocked out

00:15:15.449 --> 00:15:22.389
the administration, his car dealership couldn’t
deliver any more vehicles to their new owners.

00:15:22.389 --> 00:15:35.920
After that, business slowly resumed — but
almost one year later, he is still feeling

00:15:35.920 --> 00:15:40.820
the consequences.

00:15:40.820 --> 00:15:46.579
To prevent damages like that, how can communities
get better at protecting their systems?

00:15:46.579 --> 00:15:51.709
The experts we talked to agreed that it’s
time to move on from some outdated ideas about

00:15:51.709 --> 00:15:54.679
cyber security:
“The classic model would be almost like

00:15:54.679 --> 00:15:56.709
a castle with a moat.

00:15:56.709 --> 00:16:00.040
Everything inside would be precious and everything
outside would be malicious.

00:16:00.040 --> 00:16:05.189
This is a model that doesn’t work in the
post-Covid age, where we are all connecting

00:16:05.189 --> 00:16:06.189
from everywhere.

00:16:06.189 --> 00:16:10.369
“The question is no more, will an attacker
get in, because everyone knows: An attacker

00:16:10.369 --> 00:16:11.369
will get in.

00:16:11.369 --> 00:16:17.119
You have to protect yourself by finding out
when someone gets in and, in the best case,

00:16:17.119 --> 00:16:22.579
finding that out at a very early stage
Cyber protection today is less about protecting

00:16:22.579 --> 00:16:27.559
the wall of your castle — rather, it’s
about having guards inside of it, who stand

00:16:27.559 --> 00:16:30.249
ready to fight intruders and kick them out.

00:16:30.249 --> 00:16:34.040
That’s how you can retain some control,
even when you’re under attack.

00:16:34.040 --> 00:16:35.429
And this matters.

00:16:35.429 --> 00:16:41.690
Because those who experienced losing complete
control over their systems say that it leaves

00:16:41.690 --> 00:17:22.410
its marks:
It took the district almost a year to get

00:17:22.410 --> 00:17:25.610
everything running again.

00:17:25.610 --> 00:17:30.240
And something else was damaged during the
attack — something that is even harder to

00:17:30.240 --> 00:17:36.360
fix: The trust people have in their administration
and the confidence that the state will be

00:17:36.360 --> 00:17:42.570
there when they need it.

00:17:42.570 --> 00:17:47.460
And what if Anhalt-Bitterfeld is only the
canary in the coal mine?

00:17:47.460 --> 00:17:52.790
What if what happened here only foreshadows
what’s yet to come for others?

00:17:52.790 --> 00:17:58.090
As technology is becoming ever more sophisticated,
cyber attackers will find ever more ways of

00:17:58.090 --> 00:18:00.570
taking computer systems hostage.

00:18:00.570 --> 00:18:06.049
That is why, to prevent the worst-case scenario
— an attack that would paralyze an entire

00:18:06.049 --> 00:18:11.630
country — governments must tackle the problem
at its root: Critical infrastructure must

00:18:11.630 --> 00:18:14.920
be protected by state-of-the-art safeguards.

00:18:14.920 --> 00:18:18.610
For criminals, it must become harder to profit
from hacking.

00:18:18.610 --> 00:18:24.019
And, perhaps most importantly: Countries need
to invest in training a new generation of

00:18:24.019 --> 00:18:26.000
cybersecurity experts.

00:18:26.000 --> 00:18:29.070
This will take time, money and patience.

00:18:29.070 --> 00:18:34.019
But it’s worth it: Because the threat of
cyber attacks is here to stay.

